# <%= it.scope %>/event-bus

The event bus every app here uses: everything in
[`@mfeflow/event-bus`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@mfeflow/event-bus), plus the events of this company. `/react`
and `/standard-schema` are re-exported too.

## What to fill in

`src/events.ts` declares the events and their payloads in `CompanyEvents`, which the bus's `EventMap` extends. The
name is `<domain>.<event>.v<major>`, like `cart.item-added.v1`. `session.expired.v1` is a placeholder.

`configureCompanyEvents()` in `src/index.ts` sets how each event behaves (for example, `replay` gives a late
subscriber the last payload). Add a `configureEvent` line for each event that needs one.

Declare the events here rather than in each app. The apps import them from `<%= it.scope %>/event-bus`, so the types
arrive with the package; an app that declared them itself would have to import `@mfeflow/event-bus`, which it does
not depend on.

```ts
import { configureCompanyEvents, publish, subscribe } from '<%= it.scope %>/event-bus';

configureCompanyEvents();
subscribe('session.expired.v1', () => signOut());
publish('session.expired.v1');
```

## Settings only, no state

`@mfeflow/event-bus` keeps the subscribers and shares them across copies on the page. `<%= it.scope %>/event-bus` is
not a Module Federation singleton, so every remote gets its own copy of this package, which is fine while it holds
only types and a function that configures the bus. Keep it that way: no subscriber, queue or flag at module level
here. State belongs in `@mfeflow/event-bus`.
